diagnosis_text:
The inter-site path and BGP control plane are currently healthy. End-to-end data plane connectivity from pc1 (195.11.14.2) to pc2 (200.1.1.2) is verified: 0% loss and ~1 ms RTT over 10 ICMP probes. On the control plane, BGP between router1 (AS 1) and router2 (AS 2) is established and exchanging reachability: router1’s routing table contains BGP-learned 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2 (eth0), and router2’s table contains BGP-learned 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1 (eth0). Both routers report the BGP neighbor in Established with 1 prefix received.

Mechanism: With the inter-router peering up on the 193.10.11.0/24 link, each LAN is advertised and installed, so pc1 reaches pc2 via router1→router2 and replies return symmetrically.

Localization: router1:eth0 ↔ router2:eth0 (BGP session and link). Evidence shows the BGP Up/Down timers at ~00:01:32 when checked, indicating the adjacency was (re)established recently.

Root cause: No active fault at the time of investigation; the earlier outage is consistent with a transient BGP session reset or brief inter-router link bounce (adjacency uptime ~1.5 minutes), but the precise trigger is undetermined.

Corrective fix: No action needed to restore service. Recommend reviewing recent FRR and system logs on router1/router2 for a flap cause, and consider enabling monitoring/BFD on the inter-site link to detect and shorten any future transient outages. === END ===